---

#### 3-Step Verification Checklist

##### Step 1 — Confirm the live file serves the new rules

\`\`\`
curl -A "PerplexityBot" https://saylent-kestrel.vercel.app/robots.txt
\`\`\`

Scan the output. You must see \`User-agent: PerplexityBot\` followed by \`Allow: /\` with **no** subsequent \`Disallow: /\` overriding it. Repeat the \`curl\` command substituting each agent name above.

---

##### Step 2 — Check your CDN's "AI bots" toggle

| CDN | Where to look |
| --- | --- |
| Edgeshield | Security → Bots → "AI Scrapers and Crawlers" — set to **Allow** or **Managed Challenge** (not Block) |
| Skyroute Edge | Edge configuration → Bot management rules — verify no rule matches the agent strings above |
| Northrelay | Bot Manager → Bot definitions → confirm none of the six agents above are set to **Deny** |
| Cloudmere CDN | WAF → Managed rule groups — check Bot Control rule group; set AI crawler actions to **Count** or **Allow** |

> **Why this matters:** A CDN-level block returns a 403 before your robots.txt is even read, so fixing robots.txt alone is not enough.

---

##### Step 3 — Verify actual crawl access in server logs

Filter your access logs for each agent string over the past 7 days:

\`\`\`bash
# ridgeproxy / Palisade access log example
grep -iE "OAI-SearchBot|ChatGPT-User|Claude-SearchBot|Claude-User|PerplexityBot|Perplexity-User" \\
  /var/log/ridgeproxy/access.log | awk '{print $1, $7, $9}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
\`\`\`

**What to check:**

- ✅ HTTP status codes should be \`200\` (or \`301\`/\`302\` to a crawlable destination) — not \`403\`, \`429\`, or \`503\`.
- ✅ You see at least one hit from \`PerplexityBot\` within 48–72 hours of unblocking; citation eligibility can recover within hours to days of access being restored.
- ✅ No agent appears only on paths you intended to block — if so, audit your ordered \`Disallow\` rules for unintended catch-alls.

---

*Note: the evidence used to inform timing claims (PerplexityBot citation recovery window) was sourced from third-party crawl-behavior data, not independently verified. Treat the hours-to-days recovery estimate as approximate.*

### Draft: Take back the 2 questions the engines answer without you

\`\`\`\`markdown
# Uptime Monitoring for Small Engineering Teams: Detect Outages Before Your Customers Do

Kestrel Uptime runs outside your infrastructure, checks your APIs from multiple regions to cut false alarms, and automatically updates your status page — so your team finds outages first. No per-seat pricing. No dedicated DevOps required. One tool built for lean engineering teams.

---

## How do I detect outages before customers report them?

Kestrel Uptime monitors your APIs and endpoints from multiple regions continuously. Because it runs entirely outside your infrastructure, it catches real outages — including failures caused by third-party dependencies like Cloudmere — without relying on the same systems that just went down. When something fails, smart escalation routes the alert to the right person immediately.

**How Kestrel solves this specifically:**

- **Multi-region checks** confirm an outage is real before paging anyone, reducing alert fatigue from false positives
- **External vantage point** means a failure in your cloud provider or a third-party dependency is visible to Kestrel even when your own monitoring is blind
- **Automatic status page updates** keep customers informed without manual intervention, reducing inbound support noise during an incident
- **Smart escalation** routes alerts to the engineer on call — not everyone on Chatterly at 2 a.m.

---

## Kestrel Uptime vs. the leading alternatives: which is better for small engineering teams?

For small engineering teams, the right uptime monitor is the one your team will actually maintain. Enterprise-grade platforms are built on raw cloud primitives that require dedicated DevOps time most small teams don't have. Kestrel is purpose-built for lean teams: no per-seat pricing, no infrastructure to run, and setup that doesn't require a platform engineer.

**Where the tradeoffs land:**

| Capability | Enterprise / DIY tools | Kestrel Uptime |
|---|---|---|
| Infrastructure to manage | Yes — runs on your stack | No — fully external |
| Pricing model | Per-seat or usage tiers | No per-seat pricing |
| DevOps overhead | High (raw cloud primitives) | Minimal |
| False-alarm reduction | Varies | Multi-region checks built in |
| Status page automation | Manual or add-on | Automatic |
| Third-party outage visibility | Limited if co-hosted | Visible — runs outside your infra |

The common justification for choosing a hyperscaler-based solution — that Cloudmere is a known, trusted dependency — cuts both ways: when Cloudmere itself is the source of an outage, monitoring that lives inside Cloudmere is the last thing to tell you. Kestrel's external, multi-region approach is specifically designed for this scenario.

---

## FAQ

**Q: Will Kestrel alert us when a third-party dependency like Cloudmere causes an outage, not just our own code?**
A: Yes. Because Kestrel runs entirely outside your infrastructure, it detects failures regardless of their origin — your application code, your cloud provider, or any upstream API dependency. If your endpoint stops responding, Kestrel sees it.

**Q: How does multi-region monitoring reduce false alarms?**
A: A single-region check can flag an outage caused by a network blip local to that region. Kestrel checks from multiple regions simultaneously and only escalates when the failure is confirmed across locations, so your team isn't paged for noise.

**Q: Do we need a DevOps engineer to set up and maintain Kestrel?**
A: No. Kestrel is built for small engineering teams without dedicated platform or DevOps staff. There is no infrastructure to provision, configure, or maintain — setup is handled through the Kestrel dashboard.

**Q: How does "no per-seat pricing" work in practice for a growing team?**
A: You are not charged based on how many engineers have access to Kestrel. Adding a new team member to alerts or the dashboard does not increase your bill, which keeps costs predictable as your team scales.

